  shine but I I have to say it was pretty  easy and he he transitioned into that  role you know in some ways I found him  more at home in the director's chair  than I did in the writer producers chair  I think it was harder for him to be  writer producer and and be detached from  a lot of the decisions that were  actually being made by the director then  then it was for him to sit in the  director's chair  yeah he didn't crash the Ferrari right  you did not he really didn't he did not  the thing about this film is you guys  together put together this all-star team  behind the scenes who was the person  that was the toughest to land in terms  of whether it be Hans Zimmer or whether  it be your DP you know you would again  an all-star team the one the one that  was the most elusive to me was Hans that  was the one that when when Simon first  said look I want to try to go get Hans  quietly I thought there's no way there's  no way because he'd gone on the record  saying was going to do another comic  book movie neither of us had worked I  hadn't worked for them in a very long  time  Simon hadn't either although Simon is a  huge fan right it's written all this  green place to the tones of scores so  that seemed really beyond our reach and  it was just good fortune that that Hans  was coming through Montreal and was  playing one of his concerts so we all  went to see him play and had a meeting  with him afterwards and and he and Simon  really hit it off and then if we got  talking about the film and he sort of  understood the ambition to try to do  something more grounded in emotional  that really appealed to Hans so I would  say of everybody that was probably our  biggest gap I'm very curious I'm sure  you guys it was a very complicated  process I'm sure to design exactly how  dark Phoenix was gonna look so if he's  makeup how the fire you know all that  stuff so yeah talk a little bit about  the behind the scenes on how you decided  on the final look did it almost do  s